<section>
    <h2>The page is talking behind your back</h2>
    <p>
        Most of what a modern page shows arrives over the network after it loads: JSON from an API, filled into
        the page by JavaScript. Some of what arrives is never shown at all. A test that can read those responses
        can check data the user never sees, find out why a page shows the wrong thing, and wait for exactly the
        call it cares about.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Catch a response as it happens</h2>
    <p>
        <code>RunAndWaitForResponseAsync</code> starts listening, runs an action, and returns the first response
        that matches:
    </p>
<pre><code>var response = await Page.RunAndWaitForResponseAsync(
    () => Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Search" }).ClickAsync(),
    r => r.Url.Contains("/api/search") &amp;&amp; r.Status == 200);</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Order matters.</strong> If you navigate or click first and only then start waiting, a fast
        response can come and go before you're listening, and your test waits for something that already
        happened. Wrapping the action means you're listening before it starts.
    </p>
    <p>
        It works for a page load too: put the navigation inside, and you'll catch requests the page makes as it
        opens:
    </p>
<pre><code>var response = await Page.RunAndWaitForResponseAsync(
    () => Page.GotoAsync("https://example.com/dashboard"),
    r => r.Url.Contains("/api/profile"));</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Read what came back</h2>
    <p>A response knows its URL, status and headers, and can give you its body:</p>
<pre><code>TestContext.Out.WriteLine($"{response.Status} {response.Url}");

var json = await response.JsonAsync();          // JsonElement?
var name = json?.GetProperty("user").GetProperty("name").GetString();

var text = await response.TextAsync();          // the raw body, if it isn't JSON</code></pre>
    <p>
        Not sure which request you need? Log them all while you explore:
        <code>Page.Response += (_, r) =&gt; TestContext.Out.WriteLine($"{r.Status} {r.Url}");</code>
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Step into the wire with routes</h2>
    <p>
        Listening is half of it. <code>RouteAsync</code> lets a test answer a request itself: return fake data,
        simulate a failure, or block a slow third-party script. It's how you test the error message a page shows
        when the API is down, without taking the API down.
    </p>
<pre><code>await Page.RouteAsync("**/api/profile", route => route.FulfillAsync(new()
{
    Status = 500,
    Body = "{\"error\":\"boom\"}",
    ContentType = "application/json",
}));

await Page.GotoAsync("https://example.com/dashboard");
await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Alert)).ToHaveTextAsync("Couldn't load your profile");</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Listen to the vault</h2>
    <p>
        The vault in this floor's trap room whispers its combination as you walk in, over the wire, and never
        writes it on the wall. What you can see on the page are echoes, and echoes lie. Five seconds.
    </p>
</section>
